def r2(id,name,latin,syn,parent,layer,loc,desc,fn,clin='',system='cardiovascular',src='vessels'):
    r=rec(id,name,latin,syn,parent,layer,loc,desc,fn,clin,system=system)
    return r.replace('y(v.nervousAnatomy)','y(v.'+src+')')
CV=dict(system='cardiovascular',src='vessels')
R3=[
 r2('internal-carotid-artery','Internal carotid artery','Arteria carotis interna',['ICA'],None,'vessels','Rises in the neck from the common carotid artery, enters the skull through the carotid canal, passes through the cavernous sinus and ends by dividing into the anterior and middle cerebral arteries.','One of the two main arteries of the brain. It has no branches in the neck. Here the part inside the skull comes from the BodyParts3D scan data.','Carries oxygenated blood to the front and much of the cerebral hemispheres, and to the eye through the ophthalmic artery.','Narrowing (atherosclerosis) of the internal carotid artery is a common cause of ischaemic stroke and of brief loss of vision in one eye. A carotid endarterectomy removes the plaque.',**CV),
 r2('vertebral-artery','Vertebral artery','Arteria vertebralis',[],None,'vessels','Arises from the subclavian artery, rises through the transverse foramina of the upper cervical vertebrae and enters the skull through the foramen magnum.','Paired arteries of the back of the brain. They join in front of the medulla to form the basilar artery. Only the part near the skull is shown in this model.','Supplies the brainstem, cerebellum and upper spinal cord, and with the basilar artery the occipital lobes.','A tear (dissection) of the vertebral artery after neck injury can cause a stroke in the back of the brain.',**CV),
 r2('basilar-artery','Basilar artery','Arteria basilaris',[],None,'vessels','Runs up the front of the pons, from the union of the two vertebral arteries to its division into the two posterior cerebral arteries.','A single midline artery of the posterior circulation.','Supplies the pons, midbrain and cerebellum through its branches, and feeds both posterior cerebral arteries.','Blockage of the basilar artery can cause "locked-in" syndrome and is often fatal.',**CV),
 r2('anterior-cerebral-artery','Anterior cerebral artery','Arteria cerebri anterior',['ACA'],None,'vessels','Runs forward from the internal carotid artery, then arches back over the corpus callosum as the pericallosal artery. The two sides are joined by the anterior communicating artery.','The smaller terminal branch of the internal carotid artery. The first part is from scan data; the arch over the corpus callosum and the cortical branches are schematic.','Supplies the medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes, including the leg area of the motor and sensory cortex.','A blockage mainly causes weakness and numbness of the opposite leg.',**CV),
 r2('middle-cerebral-artery','Middle cerebral artery','Arteria cerebri media',['MCA'],None,'vessels','Runs sideways from the internal carotid artery into the lateral sulcus, then fans out over the side of the hemisphere.','The larger terminal branch of the internal carotid artery. The scan data holds only the right side; the left side here is a mirror image. Cortical branches are schematic.','Supplies most of the outer surface of the hemisphere (face and arm areas of the motor and sensory cortex, auditory and language areas) and, through small lenticulostriate branches, deep structures such as the internal capsule.','The most common artery involved in ischaemic stroke: weakness and numbness of the opposite face and arm, and language problems if the dominant side is affected.',**CV),
 r2('posterior-cerebral-artery','Posterior cerebral artery','Arteria cerebri posterior',['PCA'],None,'vessels','Curves around the midbrain from the end of the basilar artery to the occipital lobe and the underside of the temporal lobe.','Terminal branch of the basilar artery in most people. The part around the midbrain is drawn schematically.','Supplies the occipital lobe (visual cortex), the lower temporal lobe, the thalamus and the midbrain.','A blockage causes loss of vision in the opposite half of the visual field.',**CV),
 r2('superior-cerebellar-artery','Superior cerebellar artery','Arteria cerebelli superior',['SCA'],None,'vessels','Arises from the basilar artery just below its end and runs round the upper pons to the top of the cerebellum.','A branch of the basilar artery.','Supplies the upper surface of the cerebellum, part of the pons and the midbrain.','A blockage can cause unsteadiness (ataxia) and vertigo.',**CV),
 r2('anterior-inferior-cerebellar-artery','Anterior inferior cerebellar artery','Arteria cerebelli inferior anterior',['AICA'],None,'vessels','Arises from the lower basilar artery and runs to the front underside of the cerebellum.','A branch of the basilar artery that often gives off the labyrinthine artery to the inner ear.','Supplies the front underside of the cerebellum and the lower pons.','A blockage can cause vertigo, hearing loss and facial weakness.',**CV),
 r2('posterior-inferior-cerebellar-artery','Posterior inferior cerebellar artery','Arteria cerebelli inferior posterior',['PICA'],None,'vessels','Arises from the vertebral artery, loops around the medulla and supplies the underside of the cerebellum.','The largest branch of the vertebral artery.','Supplies the lateral medulla and the lower back part of the cerebellum.','A blockage causes lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome: dizziness, difficulty swallowing and loss of pain and temperature sense.',**CV),
 r2('pontine-arteries','Pontine arteries','Arteriae pontis',[],None,'vessels','Small branches that leave the basilar artery and enter the pons.','Several short branches of the basilar artery.','Supply the pons.','Small-vessel blockage causes small deep strokes (lacunes) in the pons.',**CV),
 r2('ophthalmic-artery','Ophthalmic artery','Arteria ophthalmica',[],None,'vessels','Arises from the internal carotid artery as it leaves the cavernous sinus and enters the orbit through the optic canal.','The first large branch of the internal carotid artery after the cavernous sinus.','Supplies the eye, the orbit and part of the forehead and nose. Its branch, the central retinal artery, supplies the retina.','A blockage of the central retinal artery causes sudden painless loss of vision in one eye.',**CV),
 r2('anterior-choroidal-artery','Anterior choroidal artery','Arteria choroidea anterior',[],None,'vessels','Arises from the internal carotid artery and runs back to the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle.','A thin branch of the internal carotid artery.','Supplies the choroid plexus of the lateral ventricle, the optic tract, part of the internal capsule and the hippocampus.','A blockage can cause weakness and numbness of the opposite side and a visual field defect.',**CV),
 r2('posterior-choroidal-arteries','Posterior choroidal arteries','Arteriae choroideae posteriores',[],None,'vessels','Arise from the posterior cerebral artery and run forward to the roof of the third ventricle.','Small branches of the posterior cerebral artery.','Supply the choroid plexus of the third and lateral ventricles and the thalamus.','',**CV),
 r2('thalamic-perforating-arteries','Thalamic perforating arteries','Arteriae thalamoperforantes',['thalamoperforating arteries'],None,'vessels','Arise from the posterior cerebral artery near its origin and enter the brain through the posterior perforated substance.','Small penetrating branches of the posterior circulation.','Supply the thalamus and the upper midbrain.','A blockage can cause loss of sensation, drowsiness or memory problems.',**CV),
 r2('anterior-spinal-artery','Anterior spinal artery','Arteria spinalis anterior',[],None,'vessels','Forms from branches of both vertebral arteries and runs down the front of the medulla and spinal cord.','A single artery on the midline of the front of the spinal cord. Only its upper end is shown.','Supplies the front two thirds of the spinal cord and part of the medulla.','A blockage causes loss of movement and of pain and temperature sense below the level, with touch and position sense kept.',**CV),
 r2('superior-sagittal-sinus','Superior sagittal sinus','Sinus sagittalis superior',[],None,'vessels','Runs in the midline along the upper edge of the falx cerebri, from the front of the skull to the confluence of sinuses at the back.','The largest dural venous sinus. Dural sinuses are channels between layers of dura mater with no muscle in their walls. Position and size are schematic.','Drains the superior cerebral veins and receives CSF through arachnoid granulations. It usually empties into the right transverse sinus.','Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis causes headache, raised pressure in the skull and seizures.',**CV),
 r2('inferior-sagittal-sinus','Inferior sagittal sinus','Sinus sagittalis inferior',[],None,'vessels','Runs along the lower free edge of the falx cerebri above the corpus callosum.','A small sinus that joins the great cerebral vein to form the straight sinus. Schematic.','Drains the medial surfaces of the hemispheres and part of the falx.','',**CV),
 r2('straight-sinus','Straight sinus','Sinus rectus',[],None,'vessels','Runs back along the line where the falx cerebri meets the tentorium cerebelli, to the confluence of sinuses.','Formed by the inferior sagittal sinus and the great cerebral vein. Schematic.','Carries blood from the deep parts of the brain to the transverse sinus, usually on the left.','',**CV),
 r2('great-cerebral-vein','Great cerebral vein','Vena cerebri magna',['vein of Galen'],None,'vessels','Short vein below the back end of the corpus callosum, formed by the union of the two internal cerebral veins.','A short, wide vein that drains the deep brain into the straight sinus. Schematic.','Drains deep structures such as the thalamus, basal ganglia and choroid plexus.','Malformations of the vein of Galen can cause heart failure in newborns.',**CV),
 r2('internal-cerebral-veins','Internal cerebral veins','Venae internae cerebri',[],None,'vessels','Run back in the roof of the third ventricle from the interventricular foramina.','A pair of deep veins that unite to form the great cerebral vein. Schematic.','Drain the thalamus, striatum and choroid plexus of the lateral and third ventricles.','',**CV),
 r2('transverse-sinus','Transverse sinus','Sinus transversus',[],None,'vessels','Runs sideways along the back edge of the tentorium cerebelli from the confluence of sinuses to the petrous part of the temporal bone.','Paired sinuses, usually larger on the right. Schematic position.','Carry blood from the superior sagittal and straight sinuses to the sigmoid sinuses.','Thrombosis here can raise pressure inside the skull.',**CV),
 r2('sigmoid-sinus','Sigmoid sinus','Sinus sigmoideus',[],None,'vessels','S-shaped continuation of the transverse sinus, grooving the mastoid part of the temporal bone to the jugular foramen.','Continues as the internal jugular vein. Schematic position.','Carries blood out of the skull.','Infection of the middle ear or mastoid can spread to the sigmoid sinus and cause thrombosis.',**CV),
 r2('internal-jugular-vein','Internal jugular vein','Vena jugularis interna',['IJV'],None,'vessels','Leaves the skull through the jugular foramen and runs down the neck in the carotid sheath beside the carotid artery.','The main vein of the head and neck. Only its upper part is shown here.','Carries blood from the brain, face and neck towards the heart. It joins the subclavian vein to form the brachiocephalic vein.','The jugular venous pressure is read from it to estimate pressure in the right side of the heart. It is a common site for central venous catheters.',**CV),
 r2('cavernous-sinus','Cavernous sinus','Sinus cavernosus',[],None,'vessels','One on each side of the sella turcica and pituitary gland.','A venous space that contains the internal carotid artery and cranial nerves III, IV, V1, V2 and VI. Schematic.','Drains the eye, the front of the brain and part of the face into the petrosal sinuses.','Infection of the face can spread to it and cause cavernous sinus thrombosis.',**CV),
 r2('petrosal-sinuses','Petrosal sinuses','Sinus petrosus superior et inferior',[],None,'vessels','Run along the upper and lower borders of the petrous part of the temporal bone.','Small sinuses that link the cavernous sinus to the transverse sinus and internal jugular vein. This model shows one schematic channel per side.','Carry blood from the cavernous sinus out of the skull.','',**CV),
 r2('superficial-cerebral-veins','Superficial cerebral veins','Venae superficiales cerebri',['cortical veins'],None,'vessels','Run on the surface of the hemispheres in the subarachnoid space.','Thin veins that collect blood from the cortex and empty into the nearest dural sinus. The pattern shown is schematic.','Drain the cortex and underlying white matter: upward veins reach the superior sagittal sinus, lower and back veins reach the transverse sinus, and front and lower veins reach the cavernous sinus.','Bridging veins cross the subdural space and can tear in a head injury, causing a subdural haematoma.',**CV),
 r2('cerebral-capillary-bed','Cerebral capillary bed','Rete capillare cerebri',['brain capillaries'],None,'vessels','Throughout the brain tissue, densest in grey matter.','A fine network of microscopic vessels between the smallest arteries (arterioles) and the smallest veins (venules). The loops here are drawn schematically and much larger than real capillaries, which are only about 7 micrometres wide.','Exchanges oxygen, glucose, carbon dioxide and wastes with brain tissue. Tight junctions between endothelial cells, supported by astrocytes, form the blood-brain barrier.','The blood-brain barrier keeps many drugs out of the brain. It can break down in stroke, infection and tumours.',**CV),
 r2('subarachnoid-space','Subarachnoid space','Spatium subarachnoideum',['CSF space'],None,'nerves','Between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater that covers the brain and spinal cord.','The space that holds cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) around the brain and spinal cord. It is crossed by fine strands (trabeculae) and carries the cerebral arteries and veins. Drawn as a thin translucent layer about 2.5 mm thick; in life its width varies.','The fluid floats the brain, cushions it against knocks and carries nutrients and wastes. Fluid returns to the blood through arachnoid granulations.','A ruptured aneurysm bleeds into this space (subarachnoid haemorrhage). A lumbar puncture takes CSF from the lower end of the space. Meningitis is infection of it.',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
 r2('subarachnoid-cisterns','Subarachnoid cisterns','Cisternae subarachnoideae',['cisterna magna','basal cisterns'],None,'nerves','Where the arachnoid mater bridges larger gaps between brain surfaces.','Wider pools of CSF. This model shows the cerebellomedullary cistern (cisterna magna), the prepontine cistern, the quadrigeminal cistern and the chiasmatic cistern.','Hold CSF and surround large arteries and cranial nerves at the base of the brain. CSF leaves the fourth ventricle into the cisterna magna.','Blood in the basal cisterns on a CT scan points to a subarachnoid haemorrhage.',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
 r2('choroid-plexus','Choroid plexus','Plexus choroideus',[],None,'nerves','In the lateral ventricles (body and horns), the roof of the third ventricle and the roof of the fourth ventricle.','Folds of blood vessels covered by a layer of ependymal cells. The model shows a schematic ribbon in each lateral ventricle only.','Makes most of the CSF: about 500 mL a day, so the total volume of about 150 mL is renewed several times daily.','A choroid plexus papilloma can make too much CSF and cause hydrocephalus.',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
 r2('arachnoid-granulations','Arachnoid granulations','Granulationes arachnoideae',['arachnoid villi'],None,'nerves','Along the superior sagittal sinus, mainly on each side of the midline.','Small tufts of arachnoid mater that push through the dura into the venous sinuses. Schematic positions.','Act as one-way valves that let CSF flow into the venous blood of the sinus.','If absorption fails the pressure rises (communicating hydrocephalus). They can wear small pits in the skull.',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
 r2('anterior-cranial-fossa','Anterior cranial fossa','Fossa cranii anterior',[],'skull','bones','Front part of the floor of the skull.','The shallow front floor of the cranial cavity: orbital parts of the frontal bone, the cribriform plate of the ethmoid and the lesser wings of the sphenoid. The region is cut from the scan bones by simple planes.','Holds the frontal lobes and the olfactory bulbs and forms the roof of the eye sockets and nose.','A fracture of the cribriform plate can tear the meninges, leak CSF from the nose and cause loss of smell.',system='skeletal',src='axial'),
 r2('middle-cranial-fossa','Middle cranial fossa','Fossa cranii media',[],'skull','bones','Middle part of the floor of the skull, behind the lesser wings of the sphenoid.','A butterfly-shaped hollow formed by the body and greater wings of the sphenoid and the squamous and front petrous parts of the temporal bone. The region is cut from the scan bones by simple planes.','Holds the temporal lobes and, in the sella turcica, the pituitary gland. Openings carry cranial nerves II to VI and the middle meningeal artery.','Fractures here can tear the middle meningeal artery or injure cranial nerves.',system='skeletal',src='axial'),
 r2('posterior-cranial-fossa','Posterior cranial fossa','Fossa cranii posterior',[],'skull','bones','Back and lowest part of the floor of the skull, behind the petrous ridges.','The deepest and largest fossa, formed mainly by the occipital bone and the back of the petrous temporal bones. The region is cut from the scan bones by simple planes.','Holds the cerebellum, pons and medulla. The foramen magnum, jugular foramen and internal acoustic meatus open into it.','Swelling here can block the fourth ventricle (hydrocephalus) or push the cerebellar tonsils through the foramen magnum and press on the medulla.',system='skeletal',src='axial'),
 r2('calvaria','Calvaria (skull cap)','Calvaria',['skull vault','skullcap'],'skull','bones','The upper dome of the skull above the cranial base.','The vault formed by the frontal bone, the two parietal bones and the upper occipital and temporal bones, joined by the coronal, sagittal and lambdoid sutures.','Protects the upper brain.','A skull fracture at the thin side (pterion) can tear the middle meningeal artery and cause an extradural haematoma.',system='skeletal',src='axial'),
 r2('corticospinal-tract','Corticospinal tract','Tractus corticospinalis',['pyramidal tract'],None,'nerves','From the motor cortex through the corona radiata, internal capsule, cerebral peduncle, pons and the pyramid of the medulla, then down the lateral column of the spinal cord.','The main pathway for voluntary movement. About 85-90% of its fibres cross to the other side in the pyramidal decussation at the bottom of the medulla, so each side of the brain controls the opposite side of the body. Paths are drawn schematically as thin tubes.','Carries movement commands from the cortex to the motor neurons of the spinal cord, which in turn drive the muscles through peripheral nerves.','A stroke above the decussation causes weakness on the opposite side of the body. Damage to this tract gives an upper motor neuron picture: weakness, raised tone and brisk reflexes.',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
 r2('dorsal-column-medial-lemniscus-pathway','Dorsal column - medial lemniscus pathway','Via columnae posteriores et lemnisci medialis',['DCML','posterior column pathway'],None,'nerves','Up the back (dorsal) columns of the spinal cord on the same side, to the gracile and cuneate nuclei in the medulla, where it crosses, then up the medial lemniscus to the thalamus and the sensory cortex.','The pathway for fine touch, vibration and joint position sense. Paths are drawn schematically as thin tubes.','Brings precise touch and body position information from the limbs to the opposite side of the brain.','Damage to the dorsal columns (for example in vitamin B12 deficiency or syphilis) causes loss of position sense and an unsteady gait that is worse in the dark.',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
 r2('spinothalamic-tract','Spinothalamic tract','Tractus spinothalamicus',['anterolateral system'],None,'nerves','Fibres enter the spinal cord, cross to the opposite side within a few segments, and run up the front and side of the cord and the brainstem to the thalamus and the sensory cortex.','The pathway for pain, temperature and crude touch. Paths are drawn schematically as thin tubes.','Carries pain and temperature signals from the body to the opposite side of the brain.','A lesion on one side of the cord causes loss of position sense on that side and loss of pain and temperature on the opposite side (Brown-Sequard syndrome).',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
 r2('dura-mater','Dura mater','Dura mater',['pachymeninx','outer meningeal layer'],None,'nerves','The outermost meningeal layer, lining the inside of the skull and the vertebral canal.','A thick, tough layer of dense connective tissue. In the skull it fuses with the periosteum and forms folds (falx cerebri, tentorium cerebelli) and the dural venous sinuses. Around the cord it is separated from the vertebrae by the epidural space. Drawn here as a smooth shell (tan).','Protects the brain and holds it in place. Its sinuses drain blood from the brain.','A tear of a middle meningeal artery (epidural haematoma) or of bridging veins under the dura (subdural haematoma) can compress the brain.',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
 r2('arachnoid-mater','Arachnoid mater','Arachnoidea mater',['arachnoid'],None,'nerves','The middle meningeal layer, just under the dura, spanning the grooves of the brain.','A thin, web-like membrane. Below it lies the subarachnoid space with the CSF; it is crossed by fine strands and carries the cerebral arteries and veins. Drawn here as a smooth, pale shell.','Forms the outer wall of the subarachnoid space and, through the arachnoid granulations, lets CSF pass into the venous sinuses.','Bleeding into the subarachnoid space (for example from a ruptured aneurysm) causes a sudden severe headache. Infection here is meningitis.',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
 r2('pia-mater','Pia mater','Pia mater',['pia'],None,'nerves','The innermost meningeal layer, closely covering the surface of the brain and spinal cord and following every groove.','A very thin, delicate membrane rich in small blood vessels. Drawn here as a pink shell right on the brain surface.','Supports the vessels that enter the brain and separates the brain tissue from the CSF.','Together with the arachnoid it is the site of meningitis.',system='nervous',src='nervousAnatomy'),
]
